A||||17||EdsB52||Will these wheels fit?||||1||
Z||000000||EdsB52||06-16-2001||06:30 PM||||I just bought a set of 16x8 Camaro 4th generation 93-97) wheels for $250, will they fit the Fiero offset? Do I need to run 40 series? What do I need to do to make them work. I haven't tried them yet, I just wanted to get an early heads-up before I wasted time. Thx, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||06-16-2001||06:53 PM||||  Ed, The only problem you will have (I believe) is in the bolt pattern. You might need an adapter. And that's as much as I know :cP<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000002||Formula88||06-16-2001||07:14 PM||||No. They won't fit.  Wrong bolt pattern and wrong offset.  If you can find an adapter to make them fit, I still wouldn't recommend it.||||reg||13
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||06-16-2001||07:29 PM|||| What is the Camaro/Firebird offset??<P>Peace, B2 OUT! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||06-16-2001||07:58 PM||||Oh sh!t! That was the thing I though would fit. I thought the offset would be slightly off, but the rest work. So much for impulse purchases! Anyone want to buy some good used 16x8 Camaro wheels?! So is the Fiero pattern smaller or larger? I wonder if it would be practicable to have new holes drilled in the wheel by a machine shop? Are the holes cold worked or heat treated after drilling? Would it be possible to buy rotors that would fit the Fiero and fit the wheels? TIA.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||06-16-2001||09:21 PM||||Won't work. The Camaro offset is about 30mm. If you use a spacer, the offset will go down to about 10mm.||||reg||1
Z||000006||qwikgta||06-16-2001||09:40 PM||||Eds.. Remember to use the search on this forum,  If you search for "wheels that fit" or something like that you will find a few threads on what Front Wheel Drive (FWD) rims fit the fiero.  There are many out there that fit, just remember a good rule of thumb for the fiero is that it needs FWD rims.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents. <A HREF="http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html</A>  <A HREF="http://vafiero.com" TARGET=_blank>http://vafiero.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by qwikgta (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||06-16-2001||10:49 PM||||Here is a list from the Fiero@yahoo.com list. I don't know who made it up or how accurate it is. It's a place to start.<BR>**BUICK***<BR> SKYHAWK, SKYLARK, 88-UP<BR> SOMERSET ALL<BR> ***CADILLAC***<BR> CIMARRON 81-88<BR> ***CHEVY***<BR> BERETTA, CORSICA 87-97<BR> GTU, GTZ, Z-26 89-97<BR> CAVALIER 81-UP<BR> Z24 89-UP<BR> CELEBRITY 82-90<BR> CITATION 80-85<BR> X-11 81-85<BR> MALIBU, CHEVELLE 97-UP<BR> ***CHRYSLER***<BR> LEBARON 86-95<BR> LASER, GTS 85-88<BR> NEW YORKER 86-93<BR> SEBRING CONVERTIBLE 96-UP<BR> ***DODGE***<BR> ARIES, 400, 600 86-88<BR> DAYTONA, TURBO Z 85-93<BR> DYNASTY 88-93<BR> LANCER 85-89<BR> SHADOW 87-94<BR> SPIRIT 89-94<BR> STRATUS 95-UP<BR> ***LEXUS***<BR> ES, 250 90-93<BR> ***OLDSMOBILE***<BR> ACHIEVA 92-97<BR> CUTLASS CALAIS, 82-89<BR> CIERA, FIRENZA<BR> ***PLYMOUTH***<BR> ACCLAIM 89-95<BR> BREEZE 95-UP<BR> CARAVELLE 85-88<BR> LASER 85-89<BR> RS, TURBO 85-89<BR> NEON 5-LUG 95-99<BR> RELIANT, TOURISMO 85-89<BR> SUNDANCE 87-94<BR> ***PONTIAC***<BR> 6000, LE 82-90<BR> FIERO, GT 84-88<BR> GRANDAM 85-98<BR> SE, GT 85-98<BR> SUNBIRD, J2000 82-94<BR> SUNFIRE GT 95-UP<BR> ***TOYOTA***<BR> CAMRY 86-91<BR> CELICA 92-98<BR> ***VOLKSWAGEN***<BR> BEETLE 99-UP<BR> CORRADO VR6 92-93<BR> JETTAIII (4CYL) 99-UP<BR> JETTAIII VR6 93-UP<BR> GLX VR6 92-98<P> OTHER WHEELS<BR> ***DODGE TRUCKS***<BR> CARAVAN 86-95<BR> ***PLYMOUTH TRUCKS***<BR> VOYAGER 86-96<BR> ***SUBARU***<BR> FORESTER 97-UP<BR> IMPREZA 93-UP<BR> LEGACY 90-UP<BR> OUTBACK 95-UP<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||06-17-2001||02:18 AM||||I might be way out there, but is it possible to drill holes for the lugs inbetween the existing ones? I've seen universal wheels that allow for this. What would be the possibility or cost of having this proffesionally done? Thx, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||EdsB52||06-17-2001||02:58 PM||||x<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||06-17-2001||03:34 PM||||The offset is about 2 inches off so you would have to get some wheel spacers. With wheel spacers that big, they would put a set of studs in the spacers themselves. Since they are doing that, you could have them put the new studs in the Camaro bolt pattern and have the holes drilled to bolt the adapters onto the Fiero hubs. Of course the adapters would end up costing more than the wheels did.||||reg||1
Z||000011||EdsB52||06-17-2001||07:26 PM||||That's a smokin idea. Do you really think the spacers would cost that much? Would having spacers that thick create any stering geometry problems? Would the spacers be steel or aluminum, or either? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Philphine||06-17-2001||10:40 PM||||i saw one car that shouldn't be on that list i think. pontiac 6000s have a 5x4.5" pattern, or so i was told. thats what i want to convert to on one car.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||06-18-2001||10:57 AM||||Spacers won't help. They will move your offset in the wrong direction. The Camaro wheels already have the centerline too far out. Adding spacers puts it out even farther.  It is a very bad idea to drill new holes in the wheel hubs too. There is one guy who has re-drilled and added a specially fabricated backing plate to '88 hubs to allow the use of Porsche Boxster wheels. They have the same bolt pattern as the Camaro, but lots more offset. Personally, I have to question the safety of this mod, particularly on the front. You can read about it here.  <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||06-18-2001||01:10 PM||||Newer Camaros (93 and later) have a deeper backspacing than earlier ones, Putting a spacer on it will move the mounting point inward resulting in less backspacing.<BR>I looked at the site with the guy and the Porsche rims. I would want more metal than he had left on those hubs. Just in case.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||EdsB52||06-18-2001||01:18 PM||||Thanks for that link, it was real helpfull. So, sounds like I can do it if I'm willing to invest a year of my life into it!         <P>"Spacers won't help. They will move your offset in the wrong direction. The Camaro wheels already have the centerline too far out. Adding spacers puts it out even farther."<P>What exactly does this mean? Does it mean that even if I was able to bolt the Camaro wheels on without spacers, they would stick out? Thanks for all the input.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||06-18-2001||03:38 PM||||If you could bolt the Camaro wheels on now they would stick out. If you put spacers under them, they would stick out further.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||06-18-2001||03:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>i saw one car that shouldn't be on that list i think. pontiac 6000s have a 5x4.5" pattern, or so i was told. thats what i want to convert to on one car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The wagons at least have the 5 x 115 bolt circle. I don't know if the non-wagon bodies have the 5 x 100 circle or not.<P>The rear of an 84-87 Fiero is eay to convert to 5 x 115: just install Pontiac 6000 wagon (or equivalent) front hub carriers, outer CV joints, bearing hubs, and brakes (10" diameter vented 1" thick!)<P>||||reg||1
